  "aboutTarotHeading1": "A card game, first",
  "@aboutTarotHeading1": {
    "description": "First section heading on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotBody1": "Tarot was born in 15th-century Italy as an ordinary trick-taking card game, tarocchi, played by noble families like the Visconti-Sforza — some of the oldest surviving decks were commissioned by them, hand-painted. At the time there was nothing esoteric about it: it was a card game, similar in spirit to bridge.",
  "@aboutTarotBody1": {
    "description": "First section body text on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotHeading2": "The mystical turn",
  "@aboutTarotHeading2": {
    "description": "Second section heading on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotBody2": "The association with the occult came much later, at the end of the 18th century, when the French occultist Antoine Court de Gébelin spread the idea — with no real historical basis — that tarot came from Ancient Egypt and the Book of Thoth. The legend caught on, even though it was false.",
  "@aboutTarotBody2": {
    "description": "Second section body text on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotHeading3": "The esoteric systemization",
  "@aboutTarotHeading3": {
    "description": "Third section heading on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotBody3": "In the 19th century, the Golden Dawn — an English esoteric order — established tarot as a divination and magical symbolism tool, building much of the system of correspondences (elements, astrology, kabbalah) still used today.",
  "@aboutTarotBody3": {
    "description": "Third section body text on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotHeading4": "The deck that became the standard",
  "@aboutTarotHeading4": {
    "description": "Fourth section heading on the About Tarot screen."
  },
  "aboutTarotBody4": "In 1909 the Rider-Waite-Smith deck was published, illustrated by Pamela Colman Smith under the direction of Arthur Edward Waite. It gave each of the 78 cards — not just the 22 of the major arcana — an illustrated scene with its own meaning, which made it far easier to read than earlier decks. That's the visual and symbolic standard this app follows, and the one most modern apps and decks still use today.",
  "@aboutTarotBody4": {
    "description": "Fourth section body text on the About Tarot screen."
  },

